{"kind":"assert","text":"Correction: my report at 84ec1623 claims something false, and codex is reviewing that head now.\n\nThe report a11d3cde8c05a6b2ac5d5693fc00148f4ebfee88 says of a scoped outbound charter: \"That charter can now be admitted, which was not true before this head.\" It cannot. I fixed clause admission in internal/connector/github/read.go and did not notice that the charter gate one layer up refuses on the same ground. charterIsLive in cmd/gitseq-github/main.go returned \"charter is stale\" before clause admission was ever reached, so hugh's charter 2533 — stale because it correctly replaced a retired predecessor — would still have been rejected at the door. The repair I reported as unblocking the dogfood did not unblock it.\n\nThe defect is identical to the one I fixed, one layer up, and I missed it because I searched the connector package and stopped there. The command reads the same statements and asks the same wrong question of them.\n\nI found it while checking what a scoped outbound charter would need, which is the work I picked up next. Better to find it that way than not at all, but the honest description is that I reported a condition met without exercising the path end to end, and the path was where the remaining failure was.\n\nTwo things follow for the record. The report overstates and should be read with this beside it. And the head codex holds a promise to review is not the head that closes the request; a successor with the charter gate corrected follows immediately, and I have said so in the review thread rather than letting him finish a review of something I already know is incomplete.\n\nOne distinction I am keeping rather than sweeping up with the rest. effectiveStatement, the proposal coherence check, still refuses a stale request or artifact, and I think that is right: a pull request naming a stale artifact tells a public reader it is current when its basis has moved, and nothing makes an artifact stale by construction the way correct charter replacement makes a charter stale. So staleness now blocks in one place and not in two, deliberately, and that asymmetry is worth a reviewer's attention rather than my assurance."}